{{New Zealand Parliaments}}
The '''53rd New Zealand Parliament''' was a meeting of the legislature in [[New Zealand]]. It opened on 25 November 2020<ref>{{cite web |title=Opening of the 53rd Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/visit-and-learn/parliament-in-election-year/opening-of-the-53rd-parliament/ |website=www.parliament.nz |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=25 November 2020 |language=en-NZ |archive-date=6 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201106024642/https://www.parliament.nz/en/visit-and-learn/parliament-in-election-year/opening-of-the-53rd-parliament |url-status=dead }}</ref> following the [[2020 New Zealand general election|17 October 2020 general election]], and dissolved on 8 September 2023 to trigger the [[2023 New Zealand general election|next election]]. It consisted of 120 [[Member of parliament|members of Parliament]] (MPs)<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.parliament.nz/en/visit-and-learn/how-parliament-works/our-system-of-government/ |title=Our system of government |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=28 January 2020}}</ref> with five [[Political parties in New Zealand|parties]] represented: the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] and [[Green Party of New Zealand|Green]] parties, in [[Executive (government)|government]], and the [[New Zealand National Party|National]], [[Māori Party|Māori]] and [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] parties, in [[Parliamentary opposition|opposition]]. The [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Sixth Labour Government]] held a majority in this Parliament. [[Jacinda Ardern]] continued as [[Prime minister of New Zealand|prime minister]] until her resignation on 25 January 2023; she was succeeded by [[Chris Hipkins]].

The Parliament was elected using a [[mixed-member proportional representation]] (MMP) voting system. MPs represented 72 geographical electorates: 16 in the [[South Island]], 49 in the [[North Island]] and 7 [[Māori electorates]]. This was an increase of one electorate seat from the previous election, as a result of population growth in the North Island. The remaining MPs were elected from [[Party lists in the 2020 New Zealand general election|party lists]] using the [[Webster/Sainte-Laguë method]] to realise proportionality.<ref>{{cite web |title=MMP maths: How party vote percentages become seats in parliament |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/14-09-2017/mmp-maths-how-party-vote-percentages-become-seats-in-parliament/ |work=[[The Spinoff]] |first=Simon |last=Wilson |access-date=8 September 2020 |date=14 September 2017}}</ref>

== Background ==

=== Electorate changes ===

To achieve proportionality across electorates, there were a number of changes required to electorates based on population data determined through the [[2018 New Zealand census|2018 census]] and projected population growth.<ref name="electorate numbers and boundaries">{{cite web |title=Working out how many electorates there should be |url= https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/how-are-electoral-boundaries-decided/working-out-how-many-electorates-there-should-be/ |access-date=28 January 2020 | publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref> As such, the number of geographical electorates increased by one compared to the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 election]] to account for the [[North Island]]'s higher population growth, creating [[Takanini (New Zealand electorate)|Takanini]], and 30 general electorates and five Māori electorates had their boundaries adjusted so that each electorate contains roughly the same number of people.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ Election 2020 electorate changes: Adjusted boundaries, new names |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/08/nz-election-2020-electorate-changes-adjusted-boundaries-new-names.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807040253/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/08/nz-election-2020-electorate-changes-adjusted-boundaries-new-names.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=7 August 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |first=Zane |last=Small |date=4 August 2020 |access-date=8 September 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/historical-events/boundary-review-2019-2020/ |title=Boundary Review 2019/20 |website=Elections.nz |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=8 September 2020}}</ref>

=== 2020 general election ===
{{Main|2020 New Zealand general election}}

The 2020 general election was held on 17 October, after being delayed from 19 September due to a resurgence of [[COVID-19]] cases during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/118983011/jacinda-ardern-announces-2020-election-will-be-held-on-september-19 |title=Jacinda Ardern announces 2020 election will be held on September 19 |first1=Luke |last1=Malpass |first2=Henry |last2=Cooke |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=28 January 2020 |access-date=28 January 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=17 August 2020|title=Election 2020: Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern delays the election a month until October 17|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/122467301/election-2020-prime-minister-jacinda-ardern-delays-the-election-a-month-until-october-17|access-date=17 August 2020|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref> The dissolution of the 52nd Parliament was originally set for 12 August,<ref name="election date">{{Cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12303984 |title=Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern reveals September 19 election date |last=Young |first=Audrey |date=28 January 2020 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=28 January 2020 |issn=1170-0777 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200128021253/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12303984 |archive-date=28 January 2020 |url-status=dead}}</ref> and was delayed first to 17 August<ref>{{Cite news |title=Coronavirus: Parliament dissolution delayed, but election still set for September 19|url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/health/coronavirus/300079899/coronavirus-parliament-dissolution-delayed-but-election-still-set-for-september-19| access-date=12 August 2020|date=12 August 2020|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref> and finally to 6 September 2020.<ref name="52nd dissolved">{{cite news |last=Cooke |first=Henry |title=Election 2020: Parliament dissolves, setting stage for election campaign |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300100688/election-2020-parliament-dissolves-setting-stage-for-election-campaign |access-date=6 September 2020 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=6 September 2020}}</ref>

The 2020 election resulted in a majority for the [[Labour Party (New Zealand)|Labour Party]], winning 65 seats, allowing them to continue the [[Sixth Labour Government]] unrestricted. Their coalition partner from the 52nd Parliament, [[New Zealand First]], did not receive enough votes to pass the five percent threshold or win in an [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], kicking them out of Parliament. [[Confidence and supply]] partner the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] received 10 seats, up two, becoming the first minor party ever to increase their share of the vote following their being in government. In the opposition, the [[National Party (New Zealand)|National Party]] lost 23 seats, giving them a total of 33, and [[ACT New Zealand]] went from one seat to ten. The [[Māori Party]] won the [[Waiariki (New Zealand electorate)|Waiariki]] electorate and gained an additional list seat after losing representation in the 2017 general election.<ref>{{cite web|title=New Zealand Election Results|url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/|access-date=27 November 2020|website=ElectionResults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/|publisher=Electoral Commission}}</ref>

=== Government formation ===

Labour achieved a majority in the 2020 election, allowing them to form a government without any [[coalition]] agreements having to be made. However, Prime Minister [[Jacinda Ardern]] entered talks with the Green Party about "potential areas of co-operation" in the formation of the new government.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour kicks off Government forming talks with the Greens in Jacinda Ardern's Beehive office |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/labour-kicks-off-government-forming-talks-with-the-greens-in-jacinda-arderns-beehive-office/TIATRS37XGVRRU2VW2Y4CFCWCY/ |access-date=25 October 2020 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=21 October 2020}}</ref> After two weeks of discussions, the Green Party reached an agreement with Labour on 31 October to become part of the next Government, with co-leaders [[James Shaw (New Zealand politician)|James Shaw]] and [[Marama Davidson]] receiving ministerial positions outside of cabinet. Shaw remained [[Minister for Climate Change (New Zealand)|Minister for Climate Change]] and become Associate Minister for the Environment, while Davidson became [[Minister for the Prevention of Family and Sexual Violence]] and Associate Minister of Housing.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300146558/election-2020-green-party-votes-to-be-part-of-next-government-with-labour|title=Election 2020: Green Party votes to be part of next Government with Labour|first1=Thomas|last1=Coughlan|first2=Henry|last2=Cooke|date=31 October 2020|access-date=1 November 2020}}</ref> The new government was sworn in on 6 November 2020. Some ministerial positions changed in July 2021. Ardern was succeeded as prime minister by [[Chris Hipkins]] on 25 January 2023, following her resignation.

==Parliamentary term==
The writ for the 2020 election was returned on 20 November 2020<ref>{{cite tweet|user=ElectoralCommNZ|number=1329633612157825025|date=20 November 2020|title=The Electoral Commission has returned the writ for the 2020 General Election following the completion of the recounts in Te Tai Hauāuru and Tāmaki Makaurau. The return of the writ marks the end of the election process. More information:}}</ref> after being delayed from its original set date of 12 November due to election recounts. Under section 19 of [[Constitution Act 1986]], Parliament must meet no later than six weeks after this date; on 6 November 2020, following the new government's first Cabinet meeting, Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern confirmed that the Commission Opening and State Opening of Parliament would take place on 25 and 26 November 2020, respectively.

The 53rd Parliament is the first parliament since the [[44th New Zealand Parliament]] (and the introduction of an MMP electoral system) to have a single party hold an outright majority of seats. The Labour Party currently holds 64 seats, 3 more than the required 61 seats needed for a majority.<ref>{{cite news |title=New Zealand election: Jacinda Ardern's Labour Party scores landslide win |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-54519628 |access-date=3 November 2020 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=17 October 2020}}</ref>

=== Timeline ===
*1 November 2020 – The [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] enter into a "cooperation agreement" with [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]
*6 November 2020 –
**[[Jacinda Ardern]] is sworn in for a second term as prime minister. Other ministers are also sworn in.
**Final results of the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]] are released. Three electorates flip to Labour, and National lose two seats on the party vote, with Labour and the Māori Party picking up one each.<ref name=Judith/>
**[[Gerry Brownlee]] resigns as Deputy Leader of the National Party.
*10 November 2020 – [[Shane Reti]] is elected Deputy Leader of the National Party.
*20 November 2020 – The writ of the election is returned (having been delayed from 12 November due to a judicial recount).<ref name="return of writ">{{cite web |title=Return of the writ |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/visit-and-learn/parliament-in-election-year/return-of-the-writ/ |website=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=22 November 2020 |archive-date=20 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201120063039/https://www.parliament.nz/en/visit-and-learn/parliament-in-election-year/return-of-the-writ/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>
*25 November 2020 – Commission Opening of Parliament
*26 November 2020 – State Opening of Parliament
*20 May 2021 – [[2021 New Zealand budget|Budget 2021]] is delivered to Parliament.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/budget-2021-you-need-know|title=Budget 2021: What you need to know|first=Anna|last=Whyte|work=[[1News]]|publisher=[[TVNZ]]|date=20 May 2021|access-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130175139/https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/budget-2021-you-need-know|archive-date=30 November 2021}}</ref>
*25 November 2021 – [[Judith Collins]] is removed as [[Leader of the New Zealand National Party|Leader of the National Party]] by a caucus [[vote of no confidence]].
*30 November 2021 – [[Christopher Luxon]] and [[Nicola Willis]] are [[2021 New Zealand National Party leadership election|elected]] Leader and Deputy Leader of the National Party.
*20 May 2022 – [[2022 New Zealand budget|Budget 2022]] is delivered to Parliament.<ref>{{cite news |last=McClure |first=Tess |title=New Zealand budget 2022: Ardern offers $1bn in sweeteners to tackle cost of living 'storm' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/19/new-zealand-budget-2022-ardern-cost-of-living-storm-health-climate |access-date=19 May 2022 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=19 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220519022929/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/19/new-zealand-budget-2022-ardern-cost-of-living-storm-health-climate |archive-date=19 May 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>
*18 June 2022 – [[2022 Tauranga by-election|Tauranga by-election]].
*10 December 2022 – [[2022 Hamilton West by-election|Hamilton West by-election]].
*19 January 2023 – Jacinda Ardern announces resignation.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern resigning in February, announces election 2023 will be held on October 14 |language=en |work=[[Newshub]] |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/01/prime-minister-jacinda-ardern-announces-election-2023-will-be-held-on-october-14.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20230119004929/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/01/prime-minister-jacinda-ardern-announces-election-2023-will-be-held-on-october-14.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 January 2023 |access-date=2023-01-19}}</ref>
*23 January 2023 – [[Chris Hipkins]] is elected Leader of the Labour Party.
*25 January 2023 – Chris Hipkins and [[Carmel Sepuloni]] are sworn in as prime minister and deputy prime minister.
*18 May 2023 – [[2023 New Zealand budget|Budget 2023]] is devliered to Parliament.<ref>{{cite news |title=Budget 2023 at a glance: What you need to know |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/490161/budget-2023-at-a-glance-what-you-need-to-know |access-date=26 May 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230523221813/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/490161/budget-2023-at-a-glance-what-you-need-to-know |archive-date=23 May 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Major legislation ===
<!-- wikilink all entries to page on an Act -->
{{See also|List of acts of the 53rd New Zealand Parliament}}
On 1 March 2021, the [[Local Electoral (Māori Wards and Māori Constituencies) Amendment Act 2021]] received royal assent after being introduced by [[Nanaia Mahuta]] on 9 February. This Act eliminates mechanisms for holding public [[referendum]]s on the establishment of [[Māori wards and constituencies]] on local bodies, which allowed the public to veto a council's decision to introduce a Māori ward.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Scotcher |first1=Katie |title=Māori wards amendment bill passes final reading in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/437105/maori-wards-amendment-bill-passes-final-reading-in-parliament |access-date=23 March 2021 |work=[[Radio NZ]] |date=24 February 2021}}</ref>

On 30 September 2021, the [[Counter-Terrorism Legislation Act 2021]] passed its third reading. This Act criminalised the planning of terror attacks and expanded Police powers to conduct warrantless searches. It was supported by the Labour and National parties but was opposed by the Green, ACT and Māori parties.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=Parliament passes counter-terrorism laws, criminalising terror planning and expanding warrantless search powers |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/126530458/parliament-passes-counterterrorism-laws-criminalising-terror-planning-and-expanding-warrantless-search-powers |access-date=1 October 2021 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210930005143/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/126530458/parliament-passes-counterterrorism-laws-criminalising-terror-planning-and-expanding-warrantless-search-powers |archive-date=30 September 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 24 November 2021, the [[COVID-19 Response (Vaccinations) Legislation Act 2021]] passed its third reading. This Act allows businesses to dismiss employees who refuse to take COVID-19 vaccines.<ref>{{cite news |title=Mandate legislation pushed through Parliament amid fierce opposition |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/456455/mandate-legislation-pushed-through-parliament-amid-fierce-opposition |access-date=24 November 2021 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211124104338/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/456455/mandate-legislation-pushed-through-parliament-amid-fierce-opposition |archive-date=24 November 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 15 December 2021, the [[Resource Management (Enabling Housing Supply and Other Matters) Amendment Act 2021]] passed its third and final reading with the support of all parties except ACT. The Act seeks to address the national housing shortage by easing the process for building houses in major cities.<ref>{{cite news |title=Resource Management Act amendment passes final reading |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/resource-management-act-amendment-passes-final-reading/TJEKPQPBAUZ7S4TV65XMRAIZ5E/ |access-date=15 December 2021 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=14 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214121608/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/resource-management-act-amendment-passes-final-reading/TJEKPQPBAUZ7S4TV65XMRAIZ5E/ |archive-date=14 December 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Resource Management Act amendment passes final reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/457924/resource-management-act-amendment-passes-final-reading |access-date=15 December 2021 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=14 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214133820/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/457924/resource-management-act-amendment-passes-final-reading |archive-date=14 December 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 15 February 2022, the [[Conversion Practices Prohibition Legislation Act 2022]] passed its third reading, becoming law with broad cross-party support. The Act banned [[conversion therapy]] in New Zealand.<ref>{{Cite news|date=15 February 2022|title=Politicians react as bill to ban conversion therapy passes|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/461599/politicians-react-as-bill-to-ban-conversion-therapy-passes|access-date=21 February 2022|work=[[Radio New Zealand]]|language=en-nz|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220218050733/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/461599/politicians-react-as-bill-to-ban-conversion-therapy-passes|archive-date=18 February 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> 

On 9 March 2022, Parliament unanimously passed the [[Russia Sanctions Act 2022]], which established a sanctions-implementation regime targeting Russia in response to the [[Russian invasion of Ukraine]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Parliament unanimously passes Russia Sanctions Bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/463022/parliament-unanimously-passes-russia-sanctions-bill |access-date=11 March 2022 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=9 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220309155948/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/463022/parliament-unanimously-passes-russia-sanctions-bill |archive-date=9 March 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Russia invasion of Ukraine: Parliament passes Russia Sanctions Act unanimously |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/russia-invasion-of-ukraine-parliament-passes-russia-sanctions-act-unanimously/SGMIYENM6FUCGOXRWQYH2FILBM/ |access-date=11 March 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=9 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220309100539/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/russia-invasion-of-ukraine-parliament-passes-russia-sanctions-act-unanimously/SGMIYENM6FUCGOXRWQYH2FILBM/ |archive-date=9 March 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> 

On 18 March 2022, the [[Contraception, Sterilisation, and Abortion (Safe Areas) Amendment Act 2022]] received royal assent. The Bill creates safe spaces of about 150 metres around abortion providers and also bans obstructing, filming, dissuading or protesting against individuals seeking abortion services in those zones.<ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Dwyer |first1=Ellen |title='Safe areas' abortion law passes with large majority in Parliament |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/128080358/safe-areas-abortion-law-passes-with-large-majority-in-parliament |access-date=21 March 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=16 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220316091629/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/128080358/safe-areas-abortion-law-passes-with-large-majority-in-parliament |archive-date=16 March 2022}}</ref>

On 7 June 2022, the Government's [[Pae Ora (Healthy Futures) Act 2022]] passed its third reading. The Bill replaced the country's existing [[district health board]]s with a new Crown agency called [[Health New Zealand]] and established as separate [[Māori Health Authority]]. The Health Futures Act also established a new [[Public Health Agency (New Zealand)|Public Health Agency]] within the [[Ministry of Health (New Zealand)|Ministry of Health]] while strengthening the Ministry's stewardship role.<ref>{{cite news |title=New Health System Act passes third reading in Parliament |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/06/08/new-health-system-act-passes-third-reading-in-parliament/ |access-date=10 June 2022 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=8 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220607184346/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/06/08/new-health-system-act-passes-third-reading-in-parliament/ |archive-date=7 June 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 9 August 2022, the Government's [[Three Strikes Legislation Repeal Act 2022]] passed its third  reading, repealing the [[Sentencing and Parole Reform Act 2010]]. The Three Strikes Legislation Repeal Act removed the [[three strikes law]] from New Zealand legislation. While the bill was supported by the Labour, Green, and Māori parties, it was opposed by the National and ACT parties.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Huang |first1=Christina |title=Parliament votes to scrap three strikes law |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/08/09/parliament-votes-to-scrap-three-strikes-law/ |access-date=10 August 2022 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=9 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220810025038/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/08/09/parliament-votes-to-scrap-three-strikes-law/ |archive-date=10 August 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Neilson |first1=Michael |title=Three strikes law gone: Labour fulfils 2017 campaign promise, Nats and Act rail against move |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/three-strikes-law-gone-labour-fulfils-2017-campaign-promise-nats-and-act-rail-against-move/YOI73MBFQOCURPHDLJXJNH4E3U/ |access-date=10 August 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=9 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220809140551/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/three-strikes-law-gone-labour-fulfils-2017-campaign-promise-nats-and-act-rail-against-move/YOI73MBFQOCURPHDLJXJNH4E3U/ |archive-date=9 August 2022}}</ref>

On 13 December, the [[Smokefree Environments and Regulated Products (Smoked Tobacco) Amendment Act 2022]] passed its third reading and became law. The bill banned tobacco from being sold to anyone born after 1 January 2009, limited the number of retailers allowed to sell tobacco, and lowered the nicotine content in tobacco products.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McClure |first1=Tess |title=New Zealand passes world-first tobacco law to ban smoking for next generation |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/dec/13/new-zealand-passes-world-first-tobacco-law-to-ban-smoking-by-2025 |access-date=29 November 2023 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=13 December 2022}}</ref>

On 16 August 2023, the Water Services Entities Amendment Act 2023 passed its third reading. The bill increased the number of water services entities from four to ten, and delayed the start of the entities from 2024 to 2026.<ref>{{cite news |title=Three waters amendment bill passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/495896/three-waters-amendment-bill-passes-third-reading |access-date=18 August 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230815231754/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/495896/three-waters-amendment-bill-passes-third-reading |archive-date=15 August 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> That same day, the Government passed the [[Natural and Built Environment Act 2023]] and the [[Spatial Planning Act 2023]], the first two laws in its planned revamp of the [[Resource Management Act 1991]].<ref>{{cite news |title=RMA replacement bills pass third readings in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/495939/rma-replacement-bills-pass-third-readings-in-parliament |access-date=7 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231227011740/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/495939/rma-replacement-bills-pass-third-readings-in-parliament |archive-date=27 December 2023}}</ref>

On 23 August, the Government passed two final bills entrenching the [[Water Services Reform Programme]] ("Three Waters"): the Water Services Economic Efficiency and Consumer Protection Act 2023 and Water Services Legislation Act 2023. The first bill sets up an economic regulation regime overseen by the [[Commerce Commission]] as a watchdog over the water services entities' quality and efficiency. The second bill outlines the duties, functions, and powers of the new water services entities that would come into effect in 2026.<ref>{{cite news |title=Final Three Waters bills pass through Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/496458/final-three-waters-bills-pass-through-parliament |access-date=6 September 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=23 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230830142331/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/496458/final-three-waters-bills-pass-through-parliament |archive-date=30 August 2023}}</ref>

===Workplace culture===
In 2019, following allegations of workplace misconduct by [[Meka Whaitiri]], [[Jami-Lee Ross]], and [[Maggie Barry]] in the 52nd Parliament the Speaker of the House [[Trevor Mallard]] commissioned a review into bullying and harassment in Parliament.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2020-07-07 |title=Bullying and harassment in Parliament – one year on |url=https://www.newsroom.co.nz/page/bullying-and-harassment-in-parliament-one-year-on |access-date=2022-08-12 |website=Newsroom |language=en-AU}}</ref>

In the 53rd Parliament, Parliamentary Services started to implement the 85 recommendations from the review into workplace culture.<ref name=":1" /> Despite this, allegations of workplace misconduct continued. These allegations include:

* [[Nick Smith (New Zealand politician)|Nick Smith]] bullying his staff, with allegations from 2020 leading to his resignation in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kilgallon |first=Steve |date=2021-12-12 |title='Just a f...... secretary': Former MP Nick Smith bullied, harassed and swore at staff, according to a draft report |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127248298/just-a-f-secretary-former-mp-nick-smith-bullied-harassed-and-swore-at-staff-according-to-a-draft-report |access-date=2022-08-12 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref> 
* [[Gaurav Sharma (politician)|Gaurav Sharma]] being bullied by the Labour Whips office, the Prime Minister's office, and by Parliamentary Services.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-08-12 |title=Labour MP Gaurav Sharma: 'I stand by my claims of ongoing bullying' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/472770/labour-mp-gaurav-sharma-i-stand-by-my-claims-of-ongoing-bullying |access-date=2022-08-12 |website=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz}}</ref>
* [[Anna Lorck]] bullying her staff.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Witton |first=Bridie |date=2022-08-12 |title=Senior staffer levels bullying allegation at Labour MP |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/129559320/senior-staffer-levels-bullying-allegation-at-labour-mp |access-date=2022-08-12 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref>

The 53rd Parliament also saw four MPs referred to Parliament's Privileges Committee for various infractions:
*Education Minister and Labour MP [[Jan Tinetti]] was referred to the Committee on 8 June 2023 for misleading Parliament about [[truancy]] statistics.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=Embattled Education Minister Jan Tinetti grilled by Parliament’s powerful privileges committee |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/embattled-education-minister-jan-tinetti-grilled-by-parliaments-powerful-privileges-committee/YWHWWYXWH5GL7IXKSPRINYOPXU/ |access-date=19 June 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=8 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230613124355/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/embattled-education-minister-jan-tinetti-grilled-by-parliaments-powerful-privileges-committee/YWHWWYXWH5GL7IXKSPRINYOPXU/ |archive-date=13 June 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> In late June 2023, the Committee ruled Tinetti had not intentionally misled Parliament but had shown a "high degree of negligence." Tinetti apologised to Parliament.<ref>{{cite news |title=Jan Tinetti made error of judgement that misled Parliament, Privileges committee finds |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/492852/jan-tinetti-made-error-of-judgement-that-misled-parliament-privileges-committee-finds |access-date=29 June 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=29 June 2023 |language=en-nz}}</ref>
*ACT MP [[Simon Court]] was referred to the Committee on 20 June 2023 for disclosing the Environment select committee's vote relating to the [[Natural and Built Environment Act 2023]].<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT Mp to be investigated for sharing Committee information |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/06/20/act-mp-to-be-investigated-for-sharing-committee-information/ |access-date=20 June 2023 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=20 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230620041457/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/06/20/act-mp-to-be-investigated-for-sharing-committee-information/ |archive-date=20 June 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> On 26 July, the Privileges Committee found that Court had committed a "clear breach" of select committee confidentiality. Court apologised for his action.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT MP Simon Court committed 'clear breach' of confidentiality – Privileges Committee |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/494520/act-mp-simon-court-committed-clear-breach-of-confidentiality-privileges-committee |access-date=28 July 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=26 July 2023}}</ref>
*Transport, Workplace Relations and Auckland Minister and Labour MP [[Michael Wood (New Zealand politician)|Michael Wood]] was referred to the Committee on 18 July for failing to declare his shares in [[Auckland Airport]], [[Chorus Limited|Chorus]], [[Spark New Zealand|Spark]], and [[National Australia Bank]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Embattled Labour MP Michael Wood referred to Privileges Committee |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/07/18/embattled-labour-mp-michael-wood-referred-to-privileges-committee/ |access-date=26 September 2023 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=18 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230723182635/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/07/18/embattled-labour-mp-michael-wood-referred-to-privileges-committee/ |archive-date=23 July 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> The Committee found that he had neglected his duties but that his actions did not amount to contempt. Wood was ordered to apologise to Parliament for not managing his conflicts of interest.<ref>{{cite news |title=Michael Wood ordered to apologise to Parliament over shareholdings |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/496413/michael-wood-ordered-to-apologise-to-parliament-over-shareholdings |access-date=26 September 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=23 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230829043555/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/496413/michael-wood-ordered-to-apologise-to-parliament-over-shareholdings |archive-date=29 August 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>
*National Party MP [[Tim van de Molen]] was referred to the committee on 1 August 2023 for allegedly threatening Labour MP [[Shanan Halbert]] during a select committee hearing.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/494923/complaint-about-behaviour-of-national-mp-tim-van-de-molen-received-by-speaker |title=Complaint about behaviour of National MP Tim Van De Molen received by Speaker |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=1 August 2023 |access-date=1 August 2023}}</ref> On 24 August, the committee ruled that Van De Molen had threatened Halbert, and was in contempt of Parliament. He accepted the committee's findings and issued a public apology.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Neilson |first1=Michael |title=National MP Tim van de Molen found in contempt of Parliament for 'threatening' Labour's Shanan Halbert |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/national-mp-tim-van-de-molen-found-in-contempt-of-house-of-parliament-for-threatening-behaviour-towards-labours-shanan-halbert/YREMSCEGM5C5ZP763YRCCO7LQY/ |access-date=27 August 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=24 August 2023}}</ref>

===Dissolution===
Under section 17 of the [[Constitution Act 1986]], Parliament expires a maximum of three years "from the day fixed for the return of the [[Writ of election|writs]] issued for the last preceding general election of members of the House of Representatives, and no longer".<ref name="Constitution Act 1986">{{cite web |title=Constitution Act 1986 |url= http://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1986/0114/latest/DLM94204.html |publisher=[[Parliamentary Counsel Office (New Zealand)|Parliamentary Counsel Office]] |access-date=28 January 2020}}</ref> The writs were issued on 13 September 2020 and were returned on 20 November 2020,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=Election 2020: Governor-General signs writ, triggering next step on road to election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300106152/election-2020-governorgeneral-signs-writ-triggering-next-step-on-road-to-election |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=13 September 2020 |access-date=15 September 2020}}</ref><ref name="return of writ" /> meaning that the 53rd Parliament must dissolve on or before 20 November 2023. The [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]] is scheduled to be held on 14 October and the 53rd Parliament is scheduled to be dissolved on 8 September.<ref>{{Cite web |title=About the 2023 General Election |url=https://vote.nz/voting/2023-general-election/about-the-2023-general-election/ |access-date=19 January 2023 |website=Vote NZ |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

