===Demographics of elected MPs===
The 2020 general election saw the election of New Zealand's first [[African New Zealanders|African]] MP ([[Ibrahim Omer]]), first [[Sri Lankan New Zealanders|Sri Lankan]]-born MP ([[Vanushi Walters]]) and first Latin American MP ([[Ricardo Menéndez March]]).<ref>{{cite web|title=Election 2020: Forty newcomers include our first African, Latin American and Sri Lankan MPs|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2020-forty-newcomers-include-our-first-african-latin-american-and-sri-lankan-mps/WAU467WZ6Q2FSJ4MEUOE5BQDSQ/|access-date=19 October 2020|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ|archive-date=18 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018222938/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2020-forty-newcomers-include-our-first-african-latin-american-and-sri-lankan-mps/WAU467WZ6Q2FSJ4MEUOE5BQDSQ/|url-status=live}}</ref> Six new [[LGBT+]] MPs were elected (Menéndez March, [[Glen Bennett]], [[Ayesha Verrall]], [[Shanan Halbert]], [[Elizabeth Kerekere]], [[Tangi Utikere]]), making the New Zealand House of Representatives the national parliament with the highest percentage of LGBT+ members in the world.<ref>{{cite web|title=New Zealand is on track to elect the 'gayest parliament in the world'|url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/new-zealand-is-on-track-to-elect-the-gayest-parliament-in-the-world|access-date=18 October 2020|website=SBS News|archive-date=23 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023092255/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/new-zealand-is-on-track-to-elect-the-gayest-parliament-in-the-world|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/new-zealands-rainbow-parliament-still-has-a-long-way-to-go-in-terms-of-full-representation/3AZ5S3M5EMDRT2LCM47ICF5FJY/ |title=New Zealand's rainbow Parliament still has 'a long way' to go in terms of full representation |date=21 October 2020 |first=Jason |last=Walls |access-date=26 October 2020 |archive-date=26 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201026125353/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/new-zealands-rainbow-parliament-still-has-a-long-way-to-go-in-terms-of-full-representation/3AZ5S3M5EMDRT2LCM47ICF5FJY/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

During the 53rd parliament, 60 MPs ({{Percentage|60|120|1}}) were women—the highest number since women were first allowed to stand for Parliament in {{NZ election link year|1919}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=Women members of the New Zealand Parliament, 1933 onwards |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/visit-and-learn/mps-and-parliaments-1854-onwards/women-members-of-the-new-zealand-parliament-1933-onwards/ |website=www.parliament.nz |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=25 November 2020 |language=en |date=9 November 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2020: 'Historical high' – New Zealand Parliament readies for more diversity |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/428672/election-2020-historical-high-new-zealand-parliament-readies-for-more-diversity |access-date=25 November 2020 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 October 2020 |language=en}}</ref> The period between the swearing in of [[Soraya Peke-Mason]] and [[Tama Potaka]] was the first time there had been more women than men as MPs.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Whyte |first1=Anna |title=Women outnumber men in Parliament for first time, as new MP Soraya Peke-Mason sworn in |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130271900/women-outnumber-men-in-parliament-for-first-time-as-new-mp-soraya-pekemason-sworn-in |access-date=25 October 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=25 October 2022 |language=en}}</ref>

